A||N||23||RobertVol||Infamous WHITE SMOKE||||11||
Z||000000||RobertVol||06-04-2001||01:21 AM||||Alright well I have seen many posts on the Forum concerning white smoke. Well I have only seen this happen on my car at start up.after the first puff of white smoke at start up, I dont see any at all. What could be causing this? It seems to run good after the fact. Not losing oil. <P>Opinions<P>THanks<P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RobsGT2.jpg"> ||||reg||11
Z||000001||84Bill||06-04-2001||01:23 AM||||10 to 1 it's the valve guides||||reg||3
Z||000002||RobertVol||06-04-2001||01:26 AM||||Man! I hope not. <P>My car only has 61k mileso on it and been babied. mmm  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||06-04-2001||01:33 AM||||hehe thats just about the only reason I can think of for just one puff of smoke at startup. <BR>Unless it is just vapor that disapates kinda quick. My car puffs quite a lot when I start it but is just disapates quickly. On the other hand I had a 2.5 that would smoke at start up and if it idled for a length of time it would puff out when I steped on it. It had bad guides. Have someone start it while you stand in the rear and get a whiff.||||reg||14
Z||000004||SteveJ||06-04-2001||07:40 AM||||Not the valve guides. Its the valve guide seals. Small rubber parts that can be changed in a couple of hours by someone with some talent. Like most rubber and rubber-like parts its age and heat that gets 'em so low mileage is no guarantee they are holding up.<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||DecadenceR||06-04-2001||10:41 AM||||Rob, i was getting the same thing and as we speak, I'm replacing my valve guides and avlve seals. I'll let you know if the problem cleared up!!!<BR>Should know by sometime tonight.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RobertVol||06-04-2001||03:28 PM||||Thanks guys. <P>I think I will probably be doing that soon. (replacing the Valve guide seals) hmmm <P>Another thing to fix. Oh well. another day of beer and being out working on the car hmmm not to be bad of a weekend <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Better get planning <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RobsGT2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Butter||06-04-2001||03:43 PM||||White Smoke are you sure?  White smoke from a engine usually indicates that steam is being produced somehow.  Condensation in the exhuast or engine coolant from somewhere can cause this.  My experience with vavle guides causing smoke has been that they will put out blue smoke upon startup and then clear out. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||06-04-2001||04:39 PM||||If it's just at startup, I wouldn't do a thing.<BR>From the time I bought my 88 Firebird, it would occasionally make a big puff of smoke when I started it. Never caused the first problem. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||06-04-2001||05:10 PM||||mines done it since I installed the engine.Its been 80,000 miles now and I cant even make it use any oil between changes. brings us back to the ole 'if it aint broke , dont fix it'. Im leaving mine alone until it starts using oil or something in it breaks.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||RobertVol||06-04-2001||05:45 PM||||Yeah well you maybe right guys. <P>But I am going to be doing some other work on the engine head anyway. Maybe while I am working on it I could go ahead and change out the seals. I know alot of other things dried out when it came to seals on this car. The car was stored for many years. Thus the low mileage. But not stored properly so everything dried out even the tires has a white hue from being dried out! <P>I will say, I also thought when it came to valve seals it would be blue smoke as well. <P>But you never know<P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RobsGT2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by RobertVol (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||06-04-2001||05:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RobertVol:<BR><B>Yeah well you maybe right guys. <P>But I am going to be doing some other work on the engine head anyway. Maybe while I am working on it I could go ahead and change out the seals. <BR>But you never know<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>LOL yeah ya gotta love working on your car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Someone on here once said that doing major work to your car causes you to form a bond to it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mark||06-04-2001||06:19 PM||||I would also say it was the valve seals, IF that puff of smoke smells like oil.  By the way, I changed mine after less tan 40K miles!<BR>The umbrella seals on the intake valves seemed OK, but all the O-rings on the ends of the valve stems were hard and brittle.||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||06-04-2001||09:11 PM||||Steve is right that leaking seals themselves cause the startup smoke.  The bad seals allow the oil to seep along the stem as the eng cools and then it gets burned off upon startup.  But bad valve guides will chew up the seals much more quickly than good ones.  I've seen people make the unwise choice not to service the valve guides (while the head was apart) to save $$ and they end up having to do it later anyway when the seals are gone again. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jncomutt||06-04-2001||09:19 PM||||(sorry this used to be a duplicate of the message below)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Jncomutt (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jncomutt||06-04-2001||09:19 PM||||I have the same issue on my 84, Blue smoke at startup.  Changed the valve guide seals for $20 and cleared it right up.  I'm starting to notice an excess of white smoke whenever its colder outside.  On warmer days, I don't see it so much.  But a morning around 50-60 degrees and the engine compartment part of the car is practically disappeared.<P>Could this be a coolant leak somewhere?  I have noticed the coolant being low, but have perfectly fine oil.  Also, car runs at normal to sometimes on the cool side.  <P>------------------<BR>17 year old Fierophile<BR>'86 Coupe 160k miles<BR>'84 Mint Beauty 51k and original tires!||||reg||1
Z||000016||DecadenceR||06-04-2001||10:13 PM||||Well, I just got in from finishing up and taking my baby for a tet drive.<BR>After sitting since friday, at startup there is no smoke. None. <BR>While I ahd the head out I replaced the stock springs, guides and seals, and while I had the valves off i cleaned them with my razor knife and the dremel. Got all the deposits out. It runs so much better now.<BR>But whatever you decide to do, good luck!!!||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||06-05-2001||11:39 AM||||My car does the exact same thing. Mine are noticable worse now, it smokes when I floor it off the line also. So is it just the valve guide seals? If so, how hard are they to change? Can it be done by just removeing the valve covers, or does the head have to come off? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000018||DecadenceR||06-05-2001||12:06 PM||||It can be done just by removing the valve cover accodring to the haynes manual.<BR>but after the hard time I had getting mine to release, I'd definitely recommend taking the head off.<BR>That way you get a fresh head gasket on there, and you get to inspect the valves as well.||||reg||1
Z||000019||RobertVol||06-06-2001||07:13 PM||||OK....<P>Well another question on this.<P>I havnt looked at it yet but could the small puff of smoke be from clogged emissions systems? <P>First I noticed the smoke and now the thing is  not breathing right. You hit the gas and it seems to have a hairball. <P>Whatcha think? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||06-06-2001||07:29 PM||||Hehehe ok that sucks! I just had the heads off, and didn't have any headwork done, mainly because I was working constantly on the engine and didn't want to leave them at NAPA for the 5 days it would have taken them. Well, that and I'm broke. I took them off to replace the blown head gasket, and thats all I did really. I know I should have had a valve job, but these things cost money I didn't have. So how extremly difficult is it to do with the heads on? I am going to be removeing the intake soon to replace fuel injectors, so I will have the opportunity then. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||DecadenceR||06-06-2001||07:52 PM||||According to the book you can do it with compressed air, or you can feed chord into the spark plug hole, then rotate the cyllinders by hand until it comes up and compresses the chord...<BR>But it was SUCH pain for me to get everything off and then back on with the head sitting on my workbench. I'd HATE to have to try it while it was still on the car. Plus with the way the tool attaches to the head, it's a better angle if you can get to the back side of the head.||||reg||1
Z||000022||SteveJ||06-06-2001||08:14 PM||||Did my valve seals while on the car. Had a lot of trouble getting the locks off the tops of the valves. Had to whack 'em good and hard to get 'em to release. Doing so opened the valve and released the compressed air. Compressor had to work very hard to keep up. I don't have an air dryer so it had a lot of condensed water in the line as a result of constant operation. Sure did stop the oil burning on startup though.||||reg||1
Z||000023||DJRice||06-06-2001||08:26 PM||||I'm up to 152K on my V-6 and she puffs the white smoke at startup as well.  The way I see it, it adds character.  After all, anyone can have a car that runs perfect and doesnt blow smoke....but thats no fun...(j/k)||||reg||1
